      "text": "Rain is one of the most fundamental and vital phenomena on Earth, serving as the primary mechanism for distributing fresh water across the planet\u2019s surface. While it may appear to be a simple event\u2014water falling from the sky\u2014the process of rain formation is a complex, multi-stage journey driven by solar energy, atmospheric physics, and thermodynamics. To truly understand how rain forms, one must examine the complete water cycle, also known as the hydrologic cycle, which describes the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. This cycle is not a linear path but a dynamic, interconnected system involving evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection.\n\nThe story of rain begins long before a single drop falls from a cloud. It starts with the sun, the engine that powers the entire water cycle. Solar radiation heats the Earth\u2019s surface, particularly the vast oceans, which cover approximately 71% of the planet. This heat energy causes water molecules in liquid form to gain kinetic energy, allowing them to break free from the surface tension of the water and transform into water vapor, a gas. This process is known as evaporation. While oceans are the primary source, evaporation also occurs from lakes, rivers, and moist soil. Additionally, plants contribute",
